Notes: Four fail-opens, four designs, and the fix was to stop asking the question. Every
  design failed the same way because "prove this path is absent" is unbounded — an ancestor
  three levels up, a path too long for dirname, a resolution failure nobody enumerated. The
  bounded question is "did the open succeed", and it has exactly two answers with no third
  state to misfold. That is the general lesson, and it is worth more than the guard: when a
  predicate keeps producing fail-opens, check whether the question itself has an unbounded
  answer space, and delete the question rather than refine the predicate.
  The cost is real and accepted: a project with no STATUS.md now gets exit 1 rather than a
  SKIP. The failure message names that case in words so the exit is not mystifying. Callers
  are the maintainer's run-after instruction and this test suite, both in repos where the
  file exists; `prepare` creates the file rather than checking it first.
  Verified by hand before writing tests: clean file (the ONLY exit 0), file with a version,
  absent, dangling symlink, directory, unreadable file, locked GRANDparent, `-`, `x=y`, and a
  600-char path that breaks dirname. Every one behaves correctly.
